window.__labLoaded({"id":"7.3","title":"Rank-Based Tests: When Averages Mislead","goal":"Show how rank-based tests (Mann-Whitney, Wilcoxon, Kruskal-Wallis) cope with an outlier that a t-test cannot, using the lesson's app-timing, pain and teaching data.","question":"A designer wants to know whether a redesigned app helps people finish a task faster. Six people used the new design and six the old one; times in seconds are in the lesson. One old-design user took 95 seconds, maybe after a phone call. Does the new design really work faster, and how do we test that when one wild value can fool the usual t-test?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Two groups: the t-test versus ranks","text":"Task times in seconds for a new and an old app design. The old group has one 95-second outlier. Welch's t-test uses the size of every value, so the outlier hides the pattern. Mann-Whitney only uses the order of the values.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnew = [12, 15, 11, 14, 19, 13]\nold = [18, 22, 25, 17, 30, 95]\n\nt = stats.ttest_ind(new, old, equal_var=False)\nmw = stats.mannwhitneyu(new, old, alternative='two-sided', method='exact')\n\nprint(f'Welch t-test : t = {t.statistic:.2f}, p = {t.pvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'Mann-Whitney : U = {mw.statistic:.0f}, p = {mw.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for numbers.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which contain both the t-test and the rank tests.","Task times in seconds for the six people on the new design. None is extreme.","Task times for the six people on the old design. The 95 is the outlier.","Welch's t-test compares the two means. equal_var=False is the safe version that does not assume equal spreads.","Mann-Whitney compares the ranks instead of the sizes. method='exact' works out the exact p-value, best for small samples.","Print the t statistic and p-value. Welch uses every value's size, so the 95 inflates the spread.","Print U (a count of pair wins, explained next) and its p-value. Only the order of values matters here."],"meaning":"Welch's t-test gives t = -1.67, p = 0.156: not significant, because the 95-second outlier makes the old group's spread huge. Mann-Whitney only uses the order of times and gives U = 2, p = 0.0087, which is significant. The new design is faster, and ranks can see it.","out":"Welch t-test : t = -1.67, p = 0.156\nMann-Whitney : U = 2, p = 0.0087","figs":[]},{"title":"How strong is the Mann-Whitney effect?","text":"U counts in how many of the 36 new-versus-old pairs the new design had the larger (slower) time. Here that is only 2, so almost every pair says the new design was faster. The rank-biserial correlation turns this into a size from 0 to 1.","code":"n1, n2 = len(new), len(old)\nu = mw.statistic\n\nr = 1 - 2 * u / (n1 * n2)\n\nprint(f'new time larger in {u:.0f} of {n1 * n2} pairs')\nprint(f'old time larger in {n1 * n2 - u:.0f} of {n1 * n2} pairs')\nprint(f'rank-biserial r = {r:.2f}')","explain":["Count the people in each group: 6 and 6.","U is the number the test reports: in how many pairs the new time came out larger than the old.","Rank-biserial r = 1 - 2U / (n1 x n2), turning U into a size from -1 to +1. It is the Mann-Whitney effect size.","Print how many of the 36 new-versus-old pairs had the new design slower.","The other pairs are the ones where the old design was slower. The two counts add to 36.","Print the effect size, rounded to 2 decimals."],"meaning":"U = 2 means the new design was slower in only 2 of the 36 pairs; the old design was slower in 34. The rank-biserial correlation is 0.89, close to its maximum of 1, so almost every new time beats almost every old time. That is a large effect.","out":"new time larger in 2 of 36 pairs\nold time larger in 34 of 36 pairs\nrank-biserial r = 0.89","figs":[]},{"title":"Paired data: Wilcoxon signed-rank","text":"Pain scores (0 to 10) for eight patients before and after treatment. The same people are measured twice, so we test the differences. The Wilcoxon statistic is the smaller of the two rank sums (positive and negative differences).","code":"before = [7, 8, 6, 9, 7, 6, 8, 7]\nafter = [3, 1, 4, 0, 4, 7, 2, 2]\n\nw = stats.wilcoxon(before, after)\npt = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\n\nprint(f'Wilcoxon : W = {w.statistic:.0f}, p = {w.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'Paired t : t = {pt.statistic:.2f}, p = {pt.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Pain scores (0 to 10) for eight patients before treatment. The same eight people will be measured again.","Pain scores for the same eight people after treatment, in the same order.","Wilcoxon signed-rank test for paired data. It ranks the size of each person's change and checks whether the ups and downs balance.","The paired t-test on the same data, for comparison. It also works on each person's difference.","Print Wilcoxon's W, the smaller of the two rank sums, with its p-value.","Print the paired t statistic and p-value."],"meaning":"The Wilcoxon test gives W = 1, p = 0.0156: pain scores really dropped, because nearly every patient improved (only patient 6 got worse, by a point). The paired t-test gives t = 3.97, p = 0.0054 and agrees here, since there is no extreme outlier.","out":"Wilcoxon : W = 1, p = 0.0156\nPaired t : t = 3.97, p = 0.0054","figs":[]},{"title":"Three groups: Kruskal-Wallis","text":"Three teaching methods with three students each. Kruskal-Wallis is the rank version of one-way ANOVA. With groups this small the p-value is an approximation, so treat it as a guide.","code":"a = [65, 72, 68]\nb = [80, 85, 78]\nc = [55, 60, 58]\n\nkw = stats.kruskal(a, b, c)\nf = stats.f_oneway(a, b, c)\n\nprint(f'Kruskal-Wallis: H = {kw.statistic:.2f}, p = {kw.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'ANOVA         : F = {f.statistic:.2f}, p = {f.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Test scores of three students taught by method a.","Test scores of three students taught by method b.","Test scores of three students taught by method c.","Kruskal-Wallis is the rank version of one-way ANOVA. It ranks all nine scores together and compares the groups' rank totals.","Run ordinary one-way ANOVA on the same numbers, for comparison.","Print H, Kruskal-Wallis's statistic, and its p-value (an approximation with groups this small).","Print ANOVA's F and p-value."],"meaning":"Kruskal-Wallis gives H = 7.20, p = 0.0273, so the three methods differ in rank. ANOVA gives F = 38.78, p = 0.0004. With only 3 students per group both are rough guides, but they point the same way: the methods differ.","out":"Kruskal-Wallis: H = 7.20, p = 0.0273\nANOVA         : F = 38.78, p = 0.0004","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"new <- c(12, 15, 11, 14, 19, 13)\nold <- c(18, 22, 25, 17, 30, 95)\nt.test(new, old)\nwilcox.test(new, old)\nbefore <- c(7, 8, 6, 9, 7, 6, 8, 7)\nafter <- c(3, 1, 4, 0, 4, 7, 2, 2)\nwilcox.test(before, after, paired = TRUE)\nscore <- c(65, 72, 68, 80, 85, 78, 55, 60, 58)\nmethod <- factor(rep(c(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"), each = 3))\nkruskal.test(score ~ method)","expect":"Welch t = -1.6654, df = 5.0888, p-value = 0.1557; wilcox.test W = 2, p-value = 0.008658; paired V = 35, p-value = 0.01562; Kruskal-Wallis chi-squared = 7.2, df = 2, p-value = 0.02732","note":"For the paired test R reports V = 35 (the sum of the positive ranks) while Python reports W = 1 (the smaller of the two rank sums); both give the same p-value.","explain":["Task times for the new design.","Task times for the old design, including the 95-second outlier.","t.test compares the means; by default R runs Welch's version, which does not assume equal spreads.","wilcox.test on two samples is the Mann-Whitney test, which compares ranks.","Pain scores before treatment for eight patients.","Pain scores for the same eight patients after treatment, in the same order.","paired = TRUE makes wilcox.test the Wilcoxon signed-rank test on each patient's before-after difference.","Scores of nine students, three in each of three teaching methods.","A factor naming each student's method: A, B or C, three times each.","kruskal.test is the Kruskal-Wallis test of score by method."],"read":"Welch: t = -1.6654, p-value = 0.1557 (not significant). Mann-Whitney: W = 2, p-value = 0.008658 (significant). Paired Wilcoxon: V = 35, p-value = 0.01562. Kruskal-Wallis: chi-squared = 7.2, df = 2, p-value = 0.02732. The rank tests find what the outlier hides from the t-test."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Nonparametric Tests > Legacy Dialogs > 2 Independent Samples (Mann-Whitney U) and 2 Related Samples (Wilcoxon)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / time group.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 1  15 1  11 1  14 1  19 1  13 1  18 2  22 2  25 2  17 2  30 2  95 2\nEND DATA.\nNPAR TESTS /M-W=time BY group(1,2).\nDATA LIST FREE / before after.\nBEGIN DATA\n7 3  8 1  6 4  9 0  7 4  6 7  8 2  7 2\nEND DATA.\nNPAR TESTS /WILCOXON=before WITH after (PAIRED).","note":"Group 1 is the new design. SPSS uses a normal approximation by default, so the Mann-Whitney U is 2 with Sig. about 0.01 (the exact value is 0.0087), and the Wilcoxon table shows rank sums 35 and 1 with Sig. about 0.02 (exact 0.0156). Kruskal-Wallis is /K-W=score BY method(1,3) on a dataset like the one in the Stata tab.","explain":["Declare two variables for the app data: the task time and the group (1 new, 2 old).","Start of the inline data.","Each pair is a time then its group: the first six are the new design, the last six the old design.","End of the inline data.","Run the Mann-Whitney U test of time between group 1 and group 2.","Now declare the pain data: two variables per patient, before and after.","Start of the inline pain data.","Eight patients, each with a before and an after score.","End of the inline data.","Run the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, comparing each patient's before with their after."],"read":"Mann-Whitney: look at Test Statistics, Mann-Whitney U = 2.000 and Asymp. Sig. (2-tailed) about 0.010 (exact 0.0087). Wilcoxon: the Ranks table shows rank sums of 35 and 1, and Asymp. Sig. is about 0.02 (exact 0.0156). Both are below 0.05, so both tests find a real difference."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 12\ngenerate time = real(word(\"12 15 11 14 19 13 18 22 25 17 30 95\", _n))\ngenerate group = _n > 6\nranksum time, by(group)\nttest time, by(group) unequal\ngenerate before = real(word(\"7 8 6 9 7 6 8 7\", _n))\ngenerate after = real(word(\"3 1 4 0 4 7 2 2\", _n))\nsignrank before = after\ngenerate score = real(word(\"65 72 68 80 85 78 55 60 58\", _n))\ngenerate method = ceil(_n / 3) if _n <= 9\nkwallis score, by(method)","note":"ranksum is Mann-Whitney (it reports a z score and its p-value, about 0.010, and may also print the exact p-value 0.0087; group 0 is the new design). signrank is Wilcoxon (sum ranks 35 and 1) and kwallis is Kruskal-Wallis. The word() trick just enters the lesson's numbers quickly.","explain":["Clear any data in memory.","Create 12 empty rows, one per person.","Fill the time column. word() picks the n-th number from the quoted list and real() turns it into a number.","Make group = 0 for rows 1 to 6 (the new design) and 1 for rows 7 to 12 (the old design).","ranksum is the Mann-Whitney test of time between the two groups.","ttest with unequal is Welch's t-test, for comparison.","Fill the before pain scores for the eight patients.","Fill the after pain scores for the same patients.","signrank before = after is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test of the paired scores.","Fill the nine test scores.","Make a method code, 1, 2 or 3, using ceil(_n / 3) for the first nine rows only.","kwallis is the Kruskal-Wallis test of score across the method groups."],"read":"ranksum prints a z statistic and p-value (about 0.010, with an exact value near 0.0087 shown too) for the rank test; ttest prints Pr(|T| > |t|) around 0.156. signrank shows sum ranks 35 and 1 with p about 0.02. kwallis shows chi-squared = 7.200 and Prob = 0.0273."},"conclusion":"Yes, the new design is faster. The Mann-Whitney test gives p = 0.0087 and the new design was quicker in 34 of 36 pairs, even though the Welch t-test (p = 0.156) was fooled by the 95-second outlier. Rank tests resist outliers, but with only six people per design this is a small study, and we should find out why one person took 95 seconds.","practice":{"scenario":"A food-delivery firm compares delivery times (minutes) from two couriers' routes: a fast route (7 deliveries) and a slow route (7 deliveries). One slow-route delivery took 190 minutes because of a vehicle breakdown. Is the slow route really slower?","data":"fast = [32, 35, 30, 38, 33, 36, 31]\nslow = [41, 45, 39, 48, 44, 42, 190]","tasks":["Run Welch's t-test and report t and p.","Run an exact two-sided Mann-Whitney test and report U and p.","Count in how many of the 49 pairs the fast route was slower, and give the rank-biserial correlation.","Which test do you trust here, and why?"],"hint":"Use stats.ttest_ind(..., equal_var=False) and stats.mannwhitneyu(..., method='exact'). The effect size is 1 - 2U / (n1 x n2).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nfast = [32, 35, 30, 38, 33, 36, 31]\nslow = [41, 45, 39, 48, 44, 42, 190]\nt = stats.ttest_ind(fast, slow, equal_var=False)\nmw = stats.mannwhitneyu(fast, slow, alternative='two-sided', method='exact')\nprint(f'Welch t = {t.statistic:.2f}, p = {t.pvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'Mann-Whitney U = {mw.statistic:.0f}, p = {mw.pvalue:.5f}')\nn = len(fast) * len(slow)\nprint(f'pairs where fast was larger: {mw.statistic:.0f} of {n}')\nprint(f'rank-biserial r = {1 - 2 * mw.statistic / n:.2f}')\nprint('means', np.mean(fast).round(1), np.mean(slow).round(1))\nprint('medians', np.median(fast), np.median(slow))","answer":"Welch's t-test gives t = -1.45, p = 0.196, not significant, because the 190-minute breakdown drags the slow mean to 64.1 and inflates its spread. Mann-Whitney gives U = 0, p = 0.00058: the fast route was slower in 0 of 49 pairs, so the rank-biserial r = 1.00 (as strong as it gets). Medians are 33 and 44. Trust the rank test here, since the outlier distorts the t-test.","out":"Welch t = -1.45, p = 0.196\nMann-Whitney U = 0, p = 0.00058\npairs where fast was larger: 0 of 49\nrank-biserial r = 1.00\nmeans 33.6 64.1\nmedians 33.0 44.0","figs":[]}}});
